##DESCRIPTION
##  evaluating inverse trig functions with calculators only
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Trigonometric functions)
## DBsection(Inverse trigonometric functions - definitions, graphs, & properties)
## Date(September 2012)
## Institution(Mississippi College)
## Author(John Travis)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Trigonometry')
## KEYWORDS('trigonometry', 'inverse trig functions')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

$sin_inv = Compute(random(11.3, 81.7, 0.1));
$tan_inv = Compute(random(11.3, 81.7, 0.1));
$sec_inv = Compute(random(11.3, 81.7, 0.1));

$sin = Compute("sin($sin_inv*pi/180)");
$tan = Compute("tan($tan_inv*pi/180)");
$sec = Compute("sec($sec_inv*pi/180)");

Context("Numeric");
Context()->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.1,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);
Context()->functions->disable("Trig");

$sin_inv_cmp = $sin_inv->cmp(
    checker => sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $ansHash) = @_;
        return sin($student * pi / 180) == $sin;
    }
);

$tan_inv_cmp = $tan_inv->cmp(
    checker => sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $ansHash) = @_;
        return tan($student * pi / 180) == $tan;
    }
);

$sec_inv_cmp = $sec_inv->cmp(
    checker => sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $ansHash) = @_;
        return sec($student * pi / 180) == $sec;
    }
);

BEGIN_PGML
Using a calculator, determine each angle in degrees to 1 decimal place:

a) [` \sin(\alpha) = [$sin] `] implies [` \alpha= `] [_]{$sin_inv_cmp}
  
b) [` \tan(\beta) = [$tan] `] implies [` \beta=`] [_]{$tan_inv_cmp}
  
c) [` \sec(\theta) = [$sec] `] implies [` \theta= `] [_]{$sec_inv_cmp}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For your calculator, make sure you are in degree mode. 

a) Depending on the calculator, there may be a button for [`\sin^{-1}`], [|arcsin|] or [|asin|].

The result is [`\alpha = [$sin_inv]`], but due to periodicity of [`\sin x`] other solutions are possible. 

b) Depending on the calculator, there may be a button for [`\tan^{-1}`], [|arctan|] or [|atan|].  

The result is [`\beta = [$tan_inv]`], but due to periodicity of [`\tan x`] other solutions are possible. 

c) Most calculators don't have a inverse secant button, but note that you can rewrite this as:

[```\sec(\theta) = [$sec] ```] and taking reciprocals of each side:
[```\cos(\theta) = \frac{1}{[$sec]} = [@1/$sec@] ```]

and then taking the inverse cosine with the button [`\cos^{-1}`], [|arccos|] or [|acos|].

The result is [`\theta = [$sec_inv]`], but due to periodicity of [`\cos x`] other solutions are possible. 

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT(); 
